{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1101667-take-to-the-skies-the-sky-labyrinth-the-hedge-nontypical-locations","id":1101667,"title":"Take to the Skies! - The Sky Labyrinth - The Hedge / Nontypical Locations","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-08T09:58:31-07:00","id":1101667,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon3.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Take to the Skies! - The Sky Labyrinth - The Hedge / Nontypical Locations","content":"So a common theme that comes up in many of my games (who play with people utilizing air ships, and windwings, and also in 1e played with people who'd swallow dreams to be able to fly) is the concept of flight in the hedge, and how taking to the skies worked. Could you rise so high that not even the hedge walls kept you? Could you look down upon the hedge and the people beneath you, paving new paths where none were? Or would the hedge walls rise up to tower above you still - so high that to truly stand above them would be to cut yourself off from the rest of the world? Or is it different - perhaps the sky itself is a whole new realm altogether?<br /> <br /> \u200bIn my games, what I did for the sky was that I changed the very nature of the walls themselves. In the sky, the walls are still there - but the pathways are deceptively wider, and the walls not as visible on the earth. The walls are sharp winds - cold as ice and bitter, or hot as the flares of the sun. Here, you docked in a sea of stars, guided by the stars and moon while hunted by the sun - or vice versa.<br /> <br /> \u200bI called this place above the Hedge the Sky Labyrinth - a place as dangerous, if not more dangerous, than the ground itself - for at any moment one who is not prepared could cut themselves along the burning thorns, without even knowing they were there - and it's a long drop into the hedge, with no guarantee that you will fall onto the path.<br /> <br /> \u200bAnyways - I bring it up because I'm a bit curious if anyone else played in games where non-typical hedge areas were accessed. The sky being the most obvious one, but the ocean another good one, as well as the underground if you have a character who burrows. What did you do? What might you add to these little alien areas in your game? Of course, if you never played in such a game I'm still welcome to your ideas - because I plan to use all of the above. What kind of creatures would you run into? What kind of fruit? What advantages, risks, and disadvantages would these styles of travel have in the hedge, that made the normal mode of 'walking' more or less appealing in comparison?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1513-jakondite","name":"Jakondite","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-08T12:24:37-07:00","id":1101703,"title":"","content":"This is a brilliant solution to the potential issue of powers and abilities detracting from setting themes instead of reinforcing them. Your solution plays up a breathtaking wondrous setting without reducing the terror or trauma themes. Bravo!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-09T18:00:44-07:00","id":1102162,"title":"","content":"Thanks! It was a fun little concept to write, and worked well when we implemented it. I'm working on other things that are similar, and hoping to get ideas for people - in hindsight, the only 'real' area that couldn't be 'hedgy' is the sky - the ocean is easy to fill with wreckage, the underground easy to fill with roots - but that seems a bit boorish to me, when you can equally create an endless sea where the tides shift constantly and you never know where you are going to end at - or if going underground, you can play Undertale.<br /> <br /> \u200bI'm really interested though in what ideas other people might have when embracing these kinds of areas - places you would not typically go or utilize unless you did so with purpose <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1513-jakondite","name":"Jakondite","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-10T20:34:12-07:00","id":1102424,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jakondite</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1101667-take-to-the-skies-the-sky-labyrinth-the-hedge-nontypical-locations#post1101667\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So a common theme that comes up in many of my games (who play with people utilizing air ships, and windwings, and also in 1e played with people who'd swallow dreams to be able to fly) is the concept of flight in the hedge, and how taking to the skies worked. Could you rise so high that not even the hedge walls kept you? Could you look down upon the hedge and the people beneath you, paving new paths where none were? Or would the hedge walls rise up to tower above you still - so high that to truly stand above them would be to cut yourself off from the rest of the world? Or is it different - perhaps the sky itself is a whole new realm altogether?<br /> <br /> \u200bIn my games, what I did for the sky was that I changed the very nature of the walls themselves. In the sky, the walls are still there - but the pathways are deceptively wider, and the walls not as visible on the earth. The walls are sharp winds - cold as ice and bitter, or hot as the flares of the sun. Here, you docked in a sea of stars, guided by the stars and moon while hunted by the sun - or vice versa.<br /> <br /> \u200bI called this place above the Hedge the Sky Labyrinth - a place as dangerous, if not more dangerous, than the ground itself - for at any moment one who is not prepared could cut themselves along the burning thorns, without even knowing they were there - and it's a long drop into the hedge, with no guarantee that you will fall onto the path.<br /> <br /> \u200bAnyways - I bring it up because I'm a bit curious if anyone else played in games where non-typical hedge areas were accessed. The sky being the most obvious one, but the ocean another good one, as well as the underground if you have a character who burrows. What did you do? What might you add to these little alien areas in your game? Of course, if you never played in such a game I'm still welcome to your ideas - because I plan to use all of the above. What kind of creatures would you run into? What kind of fruit? What advantages, risks, and disadvantages would these styles of travel have in the hedge, that made the normal mode of 'walking' more or less appealing in comparison?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> As I Understand It, the Hedge takes many different forms, it's not always made of thorns. The Hedge is a barrier that separates Arcadia from the mortal world, similar to how the Gauntlet separates the physical and spiritual worlds; if crossing it was as easy as climbing over the top then there would be a hell of a lot more interaction between mortals and Fae.<br /> <br /> <br /> Let's say that you did try to fly your way out, for example. Well, the Hedge might decide to take the form of a dense formation of clouds with shearing winds, pelting hail or dancing lightning bolts. Anybody trying to fly back home would have to deal with extreme weather conditions and low visibility as they attempted to ride out the storm (something that is actually quite present in fairy tales). By the time you emerge on the other side, you're soaking, battered and bruised, not to mention exhausted and ready to collapse; which can explain the memory loss present in those taken through it by force.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 06-10-2017, 11:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-10T22:49:31-07:00","id":1102438,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jakondite</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1101667-take-to-the-skies-the-sky-labyrinth-the-hedge-nontypical-locations#post1101667\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So a common theme that comes up in many of my games (who play with people utilizing air ships, and windwings, and also in 1e played with people who'd swallow dreams to be able to fly) is the concept of flight in the hedge, and how taking to the skies worked. Could you rise so high that not even the hedge walls kept you?</div> </div> </div> </div>There's a bit of a flaw in the logic here: it assumes that in this place that you get to through archways and fairy rings and reflecting pools and dusty attic windows, this tangled mess of roads both wide and untravelled between the high-walled citadels of the Strangers and the storied lands of Earth, where there's a draw distance to what passes for reality and simply building a stable place to hang your hat invariably necessitates carving out a bit of real estate more than it does anything involving construction materials\u2026 to assume that this place has a consistent degree of totally open skybox between the ground and the open air in a world where the supernatural trends toward the mysterious and obscured \u2014 to assume that there is a consistent Sky in these dreamlands is folly.<br /> <br /> There are forests whose paths wind beneath thousand-league-high sequoias strung through with ivy that burns the heart and skin to touch. You could fly freely upward until your breath ran cold and never reach the top even as the tree trunks grew impossibly wider and knotholes opened up within the copper wood and the fog that haunted the heights thickened unto solidity. Even if the woods were merely kin to mortal copses, no blimp or bird could map their trails.<br /> <br /> There are crystal caves beneath the earth that crumble and flash into being with the ebb and flow of attention and desire amid the soul-drinking darkness it mars with form. You could scour the rock with the endless heat and infinite scorn of the Fae and reach the distant depths of Arcadia before you struck bedrock in such a place, though myriad infernal vistas may await and the possibility of floating continents and castles in the clouds should never be discounted. Despite the potential scope of an open area, you can seldom fly high enough that there isn't <i>something</i> in the way, even if that something is just the misty veils of the Wyrd or a very big wall.<br /> <br /> There are petty princes of moths and macaws who hunt the space above the treeline for errant climbers and would-be aviators, taking the intrusions as a challenge to their vertical superiority. Because they are able to do this, they are allowed; stepping off the path is not less of a transgression against the law of the fae if one steps onto the air or into the seas. The Thorns will have their due, one way or another.<br /> <br /> tl;dr The Hedge is the Hedge no matter where in it you go, but more outlandish and otherworldly and extreme locations are conceptually nearer to Arcadia in the first place and the paths and clearings that exist between the Thorns are seldom wide-open spaces besides \u2014 half the thematic point of the fae borderlands' layout is that you don't have a clear view of what's ahead, and that holds true even if you change the predominant medium of the journey.<br /> <br /> It's never just flying, digging, or swimming. The maze extends in all directions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-10T23:26:23-07:00","id":1102445,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1101667-take-to-the-skies-the-sky-labyrinth-the-hedge-nontypical-locations?p=1102438#post1102438\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's a bit of a flaw in the logic here: it assumes that in this place that you get to through archways and fairy rings and reflecting pools and dusty attic windows, this tangled mess of roads both wide and untravelled between the high-walled citadels of the Strangers and the storied lands of Earth, where there's a draw distance to what passes for reality and simply building a stable place to hang your hat invariably necessitates carving out a bit of real estate more than it does anything involving construction materials\u2026 to assume that this place has a consistent degree of totally open skybox between the ground and the open air in a world where the supernatural trends toward the mysterious and obscured \u2014 to assume that there is a consistent Sky in these dreamlands is folly.<br /> <br /> There are forests whose paths wind beneath thousand-league-high sequoias strung through with ivy that burns the heart and skin to touch. You could fly freely upward until your breath ran cold and never reach the top even as the tree trunks grew impossibly wider and knotholes opened up within the copper wood and the fog that haunted the heights thickened unto solidity. Even if the woods were merely kin to mortal copses, no blimp or bird could map their trails.<br /> <br /> There are crystal caves beneath the earth that crumble and flash into being with the ebb and flow of attention and desire amid the soul-drinking darkness it mars with form. You could scour the rock with the endless heat and infinite scorn of the Fae and reach the distant depths of Arcadia before you struck bedrock in such a place, though myriad infernal vistas may await and the possibility of floating continents and castles in the clouds should never be discounted. Despite the potential scope of an open area, you can seldom fly high enough that there isn't <i>something</i> in the way, even if that something is just the misty veils of the Wyrd or a very big wall.<br /> <br /> There are petty princes of moths and macaws who hunt the space above the treeline for errant climbers and would-be aviators, taking the intrusions as a challenge to their vertical superiority. Because they are able to do this, they are allowed; stepping off the path is not less of a transgression against the law of the fae if one steps onto the air or into the seas. The Thorns will have their due, one way or another.<br /> <br /> tl;dr The Hedge is the Hedge no matter where in it you go, but more outlandish and otherworldly and extreme locations are conceptually nearer to Arcadia in the first place and the paths and clearings that exist between the Thorns are seldom wide-open spaces besides \u2014 half the thematic point of the fae borderlands' layout is that you don't have a clear view of what's ahead, and that holds true even if you change the predominant medium of the journey.<br /> <br /> It's never just flying, digging, or swimming. The maze extends in all directions.</div> </div> </div> </div>Case in point: Infinite Macabre's Hedge in Space.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}